here helicopters  flying so the preparations are ongoing  people are starting to arrive and  the summit itself will kick off  tomorrow and that's when the leaders  will come together and begin meeting in  this castle in the bavarian alps a very  exclusive and beautiful location  now just very briefly julia are those  protesters expected to also move to  garmisch-partenkirchen to that location  or will they stay put in munich  there are already some protesters that  are there in gamish patency they have  started building up a camp where they  expect to camp out for the next few days  during the summit and there are protests  already organized for the days to come  they're in gamespat in kitchen and there  a lot of people that are now in munich  will also move to the town there to take  part in the protests there  all right uh julia sodelli they're  reporting for us from the theresa music  munich thank you so much  and we're joined now by luisa noibauer  she is one of the founders of germany's  friday for future climate movement and  she joins me online from berlin now  welcome to the program good to have you  with us  so  good morning before we talk about the g7  summit uh let's take a look at the  broader situation because much of europe  of course is now trying to break free  from its dependence on russian oil and  gas  the german economy minister who himself  is a member of the green party  decided to reactivate coal-fired power  plants  can you sympathize with his dilemma  well it's not his dilemma technically  it's a dilemma that you know societies  everywhere are in and especially the the  german society as we're just so  dependent on russian  gas and oil  and yes there are big questions now how  to fill the gaps um in the in the energy  systems but there's just this huge  difference between filling and gaps and  creating new lengthy long lasting  dependency on fossil fuels and their  autocrats and when you know ministers  say okay you know for now we have to  jiggle around a bit and fill it with  with some coal that might be fair enough  as long as climate demands are still met  as long as we're still sticking to a co2  budget meaning maybe you know  having an earlier collective than  planned um you know fiddling in um to to  fit in with our climate um budget right  in the long run  do you have any ideas uh any  alternatives that you could suggest  instead of reactivating coal-fired  plants so is this basically the only  solution we have right now  um i am not an energy systems expert and  um they have opinions though on what um  they are saying in germany is that for  now on for for this year and especially  for the next winter yes it is crucial  that we fill in our gas tanks and that  means for instance for a short amount of  time having more coal but  it must not mean that we open up for  instance numerous new installed lng  terminals on the german coast that we do  new drillings and that we create all  these new dependencies that drive us  even further down the road of climate  catastrophe i mean 2022 it should be  that turning point it should be that  moment where leaders everywhere decide  to turn away from fossil fuels as it  sparks climate disasters and wars it  should be the time where we look at  renewables and understand they're at  hand they are there they're ready to be  installed as soon as the political will  is there right i mean it must be very  frustrating because for many years the  friday for futures movement was gaining  momentum then came the pandemic and now  the war in ukraine this upcoming g7  summit was supposed to focus on climate  change now the focus is understandably  on ukraine do you have a feeling that uh  political leaders have completely lost  sight of the climate emergency  um honestly i don't know what political  leaders um you know are looking at and  what not but what it seems like from the  outside is that obviously many of them  don't want to or cannot understand the  emergency we are in and that there is um  you know that things are connected that  a war  fueled and and financed by fossil fuels  is part of the of the big spiral of  fossil fuel destruction that is causing  wars that is causing you know um yeah  the destruction of our livelihoods and  so reacting to fossil fuel rule with  more fossil fuels it just doesn't make  sense even on geopolitical level so for  the sake of the world we inherited that  is there for us to save the future that  is you know in in the hands of the  leaders right now and in our certainly  we must stop playing out these crises  against each other and understanding  that they're dots that should be  connected  uh last question final question because  obviously we mentioned that there are uh  protests uh preparing anyway to go ahead  uh what concrete decisions do you and  the protesters want to see at the g7  yeah that's a really good question so  first of all this summit must be a turn  away from fossil fuels this means  sticking to the agreements that have  already been made and namely ending  foreign funding for fossil fuels which  olaf schultz is about to crack down  which would be devastating it must  include  committing to a coal exit in 2030 which  g7 leaders must decide on the summit and  of course it must include actually you  know consequent and honest commitments  for loss and damage financing the global  south and of course talking about ending  the depths of the global source to  actually yeah make  proper and honest collaboration on a  global level possible  luis and neubauer one of the founders of  germany friday's the future climate  movement thank you so very much  thank you  well for germany the summit will mark  the high point of its turn at the helm  of the rotating g7 presidency the group  includes the most advanced economies  that's the united states canada the  united kingdom france germany italy and  japan  they're home to 10 of the world's  population but they account for a third  of global economic output germany holds  the rotating presidency of the group and  this meeting will be dominated by a  daunting set of crises with russia's war  in ukraine and climate change topping  the agenda but as dw's richard walker  reports these issues are nothing new for  the g7  germany's baltic sea coast 2007  angela merkel hosting one of the first  major summits of her career as  chancellor its name the g8  that's right back then it was still the  g8 a group of seven rich liberal  democracies plus russia also on board  vladimir putin was there and some of the  photos even made it look quite friendly  seen from today it looks like a parallel  universe  and yet all was not well  putin was angry about u.s plans for a  missile defense system  just a year later he would invade  georgia  other challenges were mounting too the  g8 promised to tackle climate change or  at least it sort of did in this final  declaration it promised to consider  cutting emissions by half by 2050  fast forward to 2015 and this time  merkel hosted in the mountains  the g8 was now the g7 again putin was  gone the group threw him out over the  annexation of crimea  this became the signature image it  suggested a meeting of minds in a group  now coming closer together without the  russians around  and yet russia loomed over things as the  final declaration made clear  it condemned the annexation of crimea  and said that russia must respect  ukraine's sovereignty  and there was a growing sense of time  running out on climate change with calls  for urgent and concrete action  fast forward again to today and russia's  war in ukraine will totally dominate  this year's summit  a war destabilizing europe and  potentially the whole world  the g7 will try to underscore western  unity in the face of all of this  and it will also try to tackle some of  the fallout of the war  like the food crisis and the energy  crunch  and with forest fires already hitting  near berlin this year the climate crisis  